Macedonians go to a referendum on changing the country's name
On Sunday, voters will vote on whether to change the country's name to the "Respublika North Macedonia".
Voting was organized to resolve a multi-year controversy with neighboring Greece, which has its own province called Macedonia.
Athenians have long argued that the name of their northern neighbor is a cleric to the territory of Greece, and has repeatedly opposed Macedonia's membership in the EU and NATO.
Macedonian President Georghe Ivanov, who opposes holding a referendum to change the name, said he will ignore the results of the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that the change of the name is simply the price to pay for joining the EU and NATO.
