Macedonians Vote in Referendum on Changing Country Name
The people of Macedonius on Sunday voted in a referendum on whether to change the country's “Respublika North Macedonia”, which will resolve a multi-year controversy with Grytsia, which blocked Macedonia the possibility of membership in the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, argues that the name of the northern neighbor is a claim to its territory and cheered on Macedonia's accession to NATO and the EU.
In June, the two governments entered into an agreement on the basis of the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents of the agreement argue that this change will undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority in Macedonia.
President Giorge Ivanov said that he will not vote in a referendum, and because of the boycott campaign, there are doubts whether the turnout of voters will reach the minimum 50%, who will consider the referendum incumbent.
Refreunctuary's question: "Do you support membership in NATO and the EU if you accept an agreement with Grjece?"
Proponents of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that this is the price that Macedonia, one of the countries formed after the disintegration of Yugoslavia it is necessary to pay for accession to bodies such as the EU and NATO.
"I came today to vote for the country's future, for the youth of Macedonia, so that they could live fluently under the auspices of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us," Olvera Georgievska said in Skopje.
Although the result of the vote does not bear legal obligations, enough members of the parliament said they will support it to make it decisive.
There will be two-third votes in parliament to change the name.
State Electoral Commissariat Sacred that there had been reports of violations by 13:00.
However, the turnout amounted to only 16% compared to 34% in the last parliamentary elections of 2016, when 66% of registered voters threw their ballots into urns.
"I came to vote for the sake of my children, our place in Europe," Gøse Tanevsky's 62-year-old voter in Skopje's capital said.
On September 30, 2018, in Trumica, Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his Wife Zoryka and his son Dushko cast their vote in a referendum on changing the country's name, which will pave the way for joining NATO and the European Union.
In front of Parliament in Skopje, 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov was preparing a small stage and placed chairs in front of the tents, organized by those who will boycott the referendum.
"We are for NATO and the EU, but we want to come there with a proudly raised head, and not through the service door," Kavardarkov said.
We're a poor country, but we've got dignity.
"If they don't want to take us as Macedonia, we can turn to others, such as China and Russia, and become part of European-Asian integration."
Prime Minister Zayev believes that NATO membership will bring a lot of necessary investments in Macedonia, where the unemployment rate is more than 20%.
"I believe that the vast majority will vote, because more than 80% of our citizens stand in favor of the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
He noted that the "yes" result will be a confirmation of their future.
According to a survey published last Monday by the Makedonia Institute for Political Studies, from 30 to 43% of voters will take part in the referendum, which is below the necessary turnout.
According to another survey conducted by the Macedonian TV channel Telma, 57% of respondents plan to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70% said they would vote.
In order for the referendum to be declared incumbent, the turnout must be 50% plus one vote.
Defeat in the referendum will be the first serious blow to the pro-Western government's policies from the beginning of its work in May last year.
