"Our Sea": Intra-Material Bolivia hopes that the court will pave her the way to the Pacific
Sailors are patrolling an equipped fleet headquarters in La Pasa.
Public buildings hung an ocean-blue flag.
Nautical bases from Lake Titchikaka to the Amazon are painted with the motto: "Our sea rightfully.
"We have to get it back."
In Bolivia, who does not have access to the sea, there is still a living memory of the loss of the coastline, which departed to Chile in the bloody conflict of the 19-th century due to natural resources, as well as the desire to swim again in the Pacific Ocean.
These hopes now are perhaps the strongest in decades, as Bolivia is awaiting the decision of the international court on October 1 after five years of consideration of the case.
"There is an impulse in Bolivia, there is a spirit of unity and tranquility, and, of course, it awaits a positive result," Roberto Calszadia, a Bolivian diplomat said.
Many Bolivians will observe announcing the decision of the National Union of Directorate of Science on the big screens across the country. They are hoping that the tribunal in The Hague will decide Bolivia in favor of Bolivia that after a decade of snatched conversations, Chile will be forced to negotiations on granting Bolivia a sovereign access to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic Indigenous president, who has clengued against his re-election next year, also places high-profile hopes for a decision to be announced on Monday.
"We are very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean," he promised at the end of August.
However, some analysts believe that the court is unlikely to decide in favor of Bolivia, and if this happens, little will change.
The body of the United Nations, based in the Netherlands, does not have the authority to predequite Chybig territory and noted that it will not determine the result of possible negotiations.
The fact that the decision was made only six months after the final arguments were given, indicates that the case “was not complicated”, told Pas Zarathe, a Chilean expert on international law.
Bolivia's case hasn't been promoted, for the last four years they've been able to promote rollback.
"The issue of access to the sea was used by the current Bolivian administration," Zarate said.
Maiful rhetoric of Morales blew up the rescenes of Chilean benevolence, she suggested.
Bolivia and Chile at a certain moment will prolong the dialogue, but after which it will be very difficult to hold discussions.
Twenty countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodriguez Veltze, Bolivian representative in The Hague, did not agree that the court's deciding was untrue.
On Monday, Bolivia will "extreme an opportunity to embark a new era of relations with Chile and a chance to put an end to 139 years of discrepancilicity, receiving mutual benefits," he said.
Kalsadiya also denied that Morales - still one of the most popular presidents of Latin America - used a maritime question as a political crutch.
"Bulia will never waive its right to access the Pacific," he added.
This resolution is an opportunity to see that we should leave the past behind.”
