Jon-Garden: knowledge of languages is mandatory for the citizens of the world
Independent schools of Scotland support high standards of education, and in 2018, excellent exam results have been obtained, which are complemented by individual and collective success in sports, art, music and other areas.
These schools, employing more than 30 000 students across Scotland, are represented by the Scott-Childs Council of Independent Schools. They are joking to provide the best level of service for students and their parents.
Independent schools aim to prepare students for further and higher education, their chosen careers, as well as make them citizens of the world.
"As an educational sector that can develop and implement an individual school curriculum, we see that modern languages continue to be a popular and desirable item to choose from in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you are talking to a person in a language that he understands, he perceives it by the mind.
"If you're talking to her mother's native language, she perceives it with her heart."
It's a powerful reminder that we can't only use English if we want to build relationships and credibility with people from other countries.
From the latest exam results this year, we can see that in independent schools, languages are in the leader in the rankings of subjects with the best scores.
In total, 68% of students who studied foreign languages received a higher score A.
Data collected in 74 Primary School schools showed that 72% of students received a high score A from Chinese, and another 72% of those who study German, 69% of those who study French, and 63% of those who study Spanish also received A.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland consider foreign languages to be vibrant skills that children and adolescents will undoubtedly need in the future.
Now languages as a subject of choice are presented in the same way as subjects of NTIM (scientific, technology, engineering and mathematicians), within the framework of independent school programs and in other institutions.
A survey conducted by the British Commission on Employment and Skin Skills in 2014 showed that 17 percent of the reasons why employers cannot close vacancies related to the insufficiency of language skills.
Therefore, language skills are becoming increasingly necessary in preparing young people for a future quarry.
Since more and more promising positions require knowledge of languages, these skills are necessary in a globalized world.
No Mattrique of the chosen career, if someone has learned the second language, he will have a real advantage in the future, because this is a life-of-living skill.
Opportunity to directly communicate with people from other countries automatically gives a person who owns several languages an advantage over competitors.
According to YouGov's survey, conducted among more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75% couldn't speak a foreign language well enough to support the conversation, and the French was the only language spoken by an ambiguous percentage of people (15%).
That's why investments in teaching languages are now important for modern children.
Knowledge of several languages, especially the languages of developing countries, will provide children with a better opportunity to find a full-fledged job.
In Scotland, each school will study their own languages.
A number of schools will focus on more classical modern languages, while others will teach languages, which are considered the most important to the UK from a 2020 perspective, such as Chinese or Japanese.
Responsibility to the interests of your child in independent schools, it will always be possible to choose several languages to learn them with qualified teachers.
Scott-Public non-independent schools are trying to create a learning environment that prepares children and equips them with the skills necessary to succeed regardless of what the future has prepared them.
Now we cannot denies that in the international business circle of languages continue to be vital for the country's future, so this should be reflected in education.
Modern languages should really be "skills of international communication".
Independent schools will continue to provide this choice, diversity, and perfection of Scottish youth.
Ilut bien le faire.
John Edward is the director of the Scottish Board of Independent Schools
