Garvard students take a course on how it is enough to relax
A new course at the Harvard University this year provided all its students with more sleep. This is an impetus to deal with a growing culture of cohesion of learning all night after caffeine refueling.
Teacher has found that university students number one in the world are often ignorant when it's about the very fundamentals of how to take care of yourself.
Charles Chaysler, Professor of Sleep Medicine at the Harvard Medical School and specialist at Brighama Women's Hospital, developed a course which he believes is the first such course in the United States.
He decided to start the course after a lecture on the effect of sleep deprivation.
At the end, one girl approached him and said: "Why do they say this to me now, in the last year?"
"She's reiterating that no one has ever told her about the importance of a dream that surprised me," the teacher of The Telegraph shared.
In the course, which was first introduced this year, students are emproplied to the basics of how good sleep habits help to achieve academic and athletic performance, as well as improve overall well-being.
Paul Barrayra, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's medical services, said that the university decided to introduce a course after it was discovered that students were seriously deprived of their snarl within a week.
The hourly course includes a number of interactive tasks.
In one section, there is an immean of the room in the dormitory, where students click cups, curtains, simulators and books to learn about the effects of caffeine and light, the effect of sleep scarcity on athletic performance, as well as the importance of sleep-related rutin.
In another section, the participants are told how prolonged disretribution of sleep can increase the risk of heart attacks, stroke, depression, and cancer.
Then the campus card with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
"We know that it won't change student behavior instantly.
"But we believe that they have the right to know, just as you have the right to be aware of the effects of gargarten smoking for health," Professor Chaysler added.
There is still a culture of pride for "sleeping all night", he added, and also noted that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on students mean that the problem of sleep deprivation is growing.
Sufficient sleep of good quality should be "secourtiful weapons" of the student to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety, even with gaining excess weight, since sleep deprivation translates the brain into a mode of starvation, which causes constant physical starvation, he said.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old KaliForniversian who studies chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Chasler to develop a course after enrolled in one of his other courses last year during the first year at Harvard.
He said that the course opened his eyes and inspires him to create a new course for the entire campus.
The next step, he hopes, will be an appeal to all postparticipators with a request to undergo such a training program before coming to a competitive institution.
Professor Chaysler advises students to set a reminder that it is necessary to go to bed, as well as put an alarm and be aware of the harmful effects of "blue light", which is radiated by electronic screens and LED lamps and can disrupt the circadian rhesity, which leads to problems with falling asleep.
