Almanac: Heiger counter inventor
And now the page from our almanac "Sunday Morning": on September 30, 1882, 136 years ago on this day, and time goes... in Germany, future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Gans" Gegger was born.
Geiger developed a method of detecting and measuring radioactive radiation, an invention that eventually evolved into a device known as Geiger counter.
Since then Geiger, Counting Geiger has become one of the key elements of science, as well as one of the key elements of pop culture, for example, in the 1950 film "Ronnado Bells" with Roy Rogers and Dahl Evans, who play dubious-lookers-scientists.
Man: "What is it in general?"
Rosgers: "It's Geiger's counter, which is used to retrievate radioactive minerals such as uranium.
"When you put on these headphones, you can really hear how radioactivity in minerals affects atoms."
Evence: "Well, well, it's really cracking now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But an invention that bears his name continues to live.
The new vaccine against cancer can teach the immune system to "sequate" malignant cells
The new vaccine against cancer can teach the immune system "sequence" malignant cells and kill them
Vaccine Teaches Immune System Recognize Malignant Circuits That Are Part of Treatment
The method presupposes the extraction of immune cells in the patient and their change in the laboratory
Then these cells are able to "sequate" the protein, which is common to many types of cancer. Next, these cells are re-introduced to the patient.
A trial vaccine demonstrates promiscuous results in patients with different types of cancer.
In one woman who received a vaccine that teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells, ovarian candied ovarian cancer disappeared for more than 18 months.
The method envisages the extraction of immune cells from the patient, changing them in the laboratory in such a way that these cells can "sequentize" protein common for many types of cancer (HER2), and then re-introduction of these cells to the patient.
Professor Jay Birzofski of the US National Cancer Cancer Institute, Maryland, noted: "Our results show that we received a very promising vaccine."
"HER2 "strengthens the growth of several types of cancer, including breast, ovarian, lung cancer and colorectal cancer," he explained.
Such an approach, which consists in remobilizing immune cells in patients and "learning" them to target cancer cells, has worked in the treatment of one type of leukomemia.
