U.S. Ambassador accuses China of "implementing Psychological Pressure" by "advertising Containing Propaganda"
A week after a state-owned Chinese newspaper posted on the four pages of the U.S. daily published promotional material about the mutual benefits of trade between the U.S.-Chineto-Chinety, U.S. Ambassador to China accusing Beijing of using the U.S. press to disseminate propaganda.
U.S. President-in-Off. Donald Trump Relcated last Wednesday's platite-in-client China Daily's note in Des Moines Register - the most populous newspaper of Iowa after China's accusers of trying to influence congressional elections, which will take place on November 6. China denies these accusations.
According to officials in the U.S. officials told Reuters, Trump's statements he accused Beijing of attempting to interfere in the U.S. elections, mean a new escalating phase by Washington, whose goal is to put pressure on China.
Although foreign governments can place advertising to intensify trade, between Beijing and Washington there is an escalation of a trade war, during which they have already increased import duties.
According to experts from China and the United States, at the beginning of this war the imposition of repressive duties in China was intended to dealt an impact on exporters from countries such as Iowa, which supported the Republican Party of trump.
Terry Branstead, U.S. Ambassador to China and former Iowa state governor, the largest exporter of agricultural products to China, said China has harmed U.S. workers, farmers and companies.
China, as Brunsted wrote in the Sunday edition column of Des Moines Register, "strengthens pressure by publishing propaganda advertising in our independent press."
"To disseminate propaganda, the Chinese government is using the way for the American people of the tradition of freedom of speech and the independence of the press by posting a paid advertisement in the Des Moines Register," Branstead wrote.
"Contrary to this, on street layouts here, in Beijing, you will find only certain voices of dissensive. Here you will not see a truthful reflection of what the Chinese people really think about the direction of development of the Chinese economy, which is a concern, from what we can conclude that China's media are under the reliable control of the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
He also added that "one of venerable Chinese editions offered to publish" his article, but did not name which.
