Inventor of the world's Internet plans to launch a "new Internet" in order to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World's Internet, is launching a new start-up to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The last project of the legend of the world of technology, the company Inrupt, is engaged in the development of an open-source platform Solid, whose idea belongs to Berners-Lee.
With the Solid platform, users will be able to choose where to save the data and to whom to provide access to this or that information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that Insrupt's goal is "global dominance."
"We must do it now," he said about the startup.
It's a historic moment.'
The application uses Solid technology, which will allow users to create "Personal Online Data Repository" or POD.
It can store lists of contacts, lists of current affairs, a calendar, a library of audio recordings and other tools for self-using and work.
It's like using Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Sluck and Spotify at the same time.
A unique trait of this sociable online data repository is that it fully complies with the needs of a user who can access any information.
The company calls it an "extension of personal capabilities through data".
According to Insrupt CEO John Bruce, the company aims to use the resources, processes and relevant skills so that the Solid platform becomes available to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Le, Bruce, a security platform acquired from IBM, several full-time developers hired to work on a project, and a community of volunteer programmers.
Beginning this week, technology developers around the world can create their own stand-alone applications, using tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee stated that he and his team did not negotiate with Facebook and GAGS about whether they should make a complete replacement when all of their business models are undergoing radical changes in a short period of time.
"We don't ask their permission."
In a Saturday publication on Medium, Berners-Lee wrote that Intrrupt's mission is to "provide commercial energy carriers and an ecosystem and to help protect the integrity and quality of a new network created on the basis of the Solid platform."
Berners-Lee transformed the Internet by founding the Consortium of the World Wide Web at the Massachusetts Technological Institute.
Berliners-Lee is an influential figure in the debate regarding network neutrality.
Even during the launch of Inrupt, Berners-Lee relied as the founder and director of the Consortium of the World Wide Rabbit, The Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I am very optimistic about the next era of network development," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Warn: Celebration of the Presentation to the Representative of the Church of the Order "Crost of Victoria" during the First World War
The only representative of the Anglican Church, who received the Order "Crost of Victoria" for participation in hostilities during the First World War, is honored in his hometown 100 years later.
Lieutenant Colonel Reverend Bernard Vanne received an award for participating in an attack near Belangris and Leocour on September 29, 1918.
However, he died from a sniper bullet four days later and had never learned that he received Britain's highest military award.
On Saturday, two of his grandchildren unveiled a membranous stone during a parade in Rashden, Northamptonshire.
One of his grandparents, Michael Wang, called "extremely sympathreatious" that the opening of the memorial stone took place exactly 100 years after his grandfather accomplished a feat, for which he received an award.
According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Vanne transferred his battalion through the Saint-Katen Canal "through a very thick fog and under dense fire of field guns and machine guns."
Later he hurried to the firing line and with "desperate valiant bravery" moved forward, before that, one broke into a field cannon, and destroyed three cans of an enemy unit.
Lieutenant Colonel Bunten Shot the German Sniper on October 4, 1918 - a little more than a month before the end of the war.
72-year-old Michael Wang said that his grandfather did what he himself would never be able to repetitive and what he deserves to be respected.
He and his brother Dr. James Wann laid a wreath after the parade led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Wann said he "feels very proud to take part in this parade" and added that "the courage of a real hero is demonstrated by the monastic, which so many people show him."
MMA fans didn't slept all night to watch the Bellator 206 Tournament, in return they were shown "Peppa Pig."
Imagine you don't sleep all the night, just to watch an action-pleased Belator 206 tournament, and you're refused to revise the main event.
