Entry into operation of a new German warship was again postponed
The most modern frigate of the German Naval Army was supposed to be enacted back in 2014 instead of obsolete Cold-Word War combat vessels. However, according to local media, this will not happen until at least the next year due to malicious systems and a swift increase in costs.
According to Die Zeit newspaper, received from a spokeswoman for the military, the commissioning of the ship "Rhineland-Pafalz", the main vessel of a series of the most modern frigates "Baden-Vürttemberg", was postponed until the first half of 2019.
This vessel was supposed to accompanies to the rest of the fleet in 2014, but the problems that arose after delivery were harmed by the implementation of an ambitious project.
Four Baden-Württemberg class ships, which were ordered by the Naval Office in 2007, must be replaced by obsolete Bremen class frigates.
It is understandable that they will be equipped with a powerful cannon, a set of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some technologies to reduce visibility, in particular, radar, infrared and acoustic.
Another important Feature – a longer period of technical support. It is Expected that new frigates will be able not to return to the base port for about two years.
Die Zeit journalists warn that due to constant delays, state-of-the-art warships, which were supposed to strengthen Germany's military power at sea, will also become obsolete.
Multi-suffocating frigate F125 fell into newspaper headlines last year, when Germany's navigators formally declined to enact the shipyard Blohm + Voss in Gamburg.
This is the first case of the return of the warship to a shipbuilding company after delivery.
Few is known about the reasons for the return, but the German media recalled a significant "defficiency of software and equipment", due to which the combat vessel became unsuitable for military operations.
Disadvantages of the software are especially important, since the Circuits of Baden-Württemberg-class ship consists of about 120 sailors - this is only half the number of soldiers who served on old frigates of Brahmen.
In addition, it turned out that the ship has a significant surplus, which impundereseses its operational characteristics and limits the possibilities for further modernization.
Rainland-Phaltz frigate is believed to be twice as heavy (7,000 tons) for ships of the same class used by the Germans during the Second World War.
In addition to malicious equipment, another problem was the total cost of the project, which also includes the price of crew training.
Expedited 2.2 billion euros turned into colossal 3.1 billion euros (3.6 billion US dollars).
Troubles with new frigates gained special importance in light of recent warnings about the decline of Germany's Naval Forces.
At the Beginning of the beginning of the year, Bundestag Commissioner Hans-Peter Barthels, reported that the country's Navy "remained a few ships capable of performing combat missions."
The official stated that the situation was rapidly deteriorating for a certain time, as the old vessels were decommissioned and the new ones did not arrive.
He expressed a deep pity that none of Baden-Württemberg class frigates was able to replenish the navy.
The National Trat will observe the secret life of bats
A new study, which is carried out on land ownership in the Scotch Highway, aims to establish how buzhans use the landscape in search of food.
Scientists are hoping that the obtained data will help shed light on the behavior of these unique flying mammals in order in the future to take measures to preserve them.
In Invedium Gardens in Wester-Rossi, scientists of the National Trust of Scotland will observe views such as untopic dwarf and non-topyr pigmei, as well as ears of an Ordinary and water novice.
Special registrars will be deployed in strategic places on the territory of land tenure to observe the behavior of bats during the season.
Employees and volunteers of the National Transement of Scotland will also conduct mobile research using hand detectors.
An expert analysis of all recorded sounds will help to establish the frequency and affiliation of bat screams.
Then it is planned to develop a more detailed accommodation map of the animals and to prepare a report to link the behavior of animals with their habitat.
Rob Dewar, Conservation Advocate at Scotland's National Trait, hopes that the results of the study will help determine which areas of residence are most important for bats and how they are used by each species.
This information will help to assess the effectiveness of environmental care measures (such as alkalis formation) and to determine the optimal methods of preserving forests for buzhans and other related species.
Over the last century, bat populations have declined significantly in Scotland and the United Kingdom.
They are threatened by the active construction and development of territories, which deprives their nesting sites.
Other Negative Factors Include Wielding Installations and Illumination Stories, Impregnation of languids, chemical processing of building materials, as well as attacks of domestic cats.
Bulletins aren't actually blind.
However, due to hunting at night, the ears are more effective for them to catch prey than the eyes.
They use a complex echolocation technique to detect ins insects and obstabilities on the trajectory.
In the National Trait of Scotland, which takes care of more than 270 historic buildings, 38 gardens of special importance and 76 000 hectares of land in the whole country are very serious about buzhn problems.
Ten specially trained experts regularly conduct research, nesting locations and sometimes even rescue operations.
By the efforts of the organization, even created the first and the only buzca reserve in Scotland. It is located on the territory of Triv estate in Dumfries & Galloway. Eight out of ten species of badges living in Scotland were found to be sheltered.
Manage holder David Thompson argues that this is the perfect place for them.
"There are excellent conditions for bats on the territory of the estate last," he assures.
"Here there are old buildings, a lot of gigantic trees and other contrivable living conditions.
However, we still need to learn a lot about bats. Thanks to the research that we conduct here and in other places, we will be able to learn more about what they need for thriving."
He insists that it is very important to inspect the area for bats before carrying out any technical works, because due to the unintentional destruction of one nesting site, up to 400 females with cunning can die due to unintentional destruction.
Bulletens and their nests are protected by law. Animals are forbidden to kill, torment or disturb.
Elizabeth Ferrell, an officer of the Scottish Béach Conservation Center, cheated the public to join the organization's efforts.
She said: "We still need to learn a lot about our bats. We still haven't revealed the characteristic features of populations of many species."
