The Bernaston plant, which produces Toyota models Auris and Avensis, last year produced about 15,000 vehicles, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the countries of the European Union.
"In my opinion, if Britain urgently leave the EU at the end of March, production at our factory will be stopped," said Marguin Kook, Toyota CEO in Bernaston.
Other automakers, such as Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, are also concerned about how the UK's exit from the EU without an agreement on international trade will affect.
For example, BMW stated that the production plant of the Mini model in Oxford will be closed a month after Brexit.
The main fears of automakers are due to the fact that a no-deal Brexit could cause risks to supply chains.
The Toyota production line operates in the mode of prompt delivery of parts and materials in accordance with the needs - the parts come every 37 minutes from suppliers in the United Kingdom and the EU for custom-employed cars.
If on March 29 the United Kingdom leaves the EU undebted, there may be interruptions in the supply of parts at the border, which will lead to delays and shortages of materials.
As the company notes, Toyota warehouse can accommodate parts for no more than one-of-a-day. Therefore, the production will have to be stopped.
Greg Clark noted that in terms of the organization of relations with the EU, Prepared by Theresa Mae (Chequers Plan), "separately provides schemes for avoiding problems at the border."
"We need an agreement. We need to conclude an optional agreement, which, in my opinion, will not only succeed at the current moment, but also to take advantage of the prospects that have arisen," he said during today's program on BBC Radio 4.
"Giving testimonies of Toyota representatives, as well as other auto processors, we desperately need to keep formed high-performance supply chains."
Toyota was unsaplied about how long production will be stopped, but in the long run they expect that an increase in costs will lead to a decrease in the manufacturer's competitiveness and, as a result, will force jobs to shrink.
Peter Tsularis, Chairman of the Organizing Commissariat of the Unify Trade Union at the Bernaston plant, who has worked here for 24 years, said that other participants were extremely concerned: "Out of experience, I can say that reduced jobs are not renewed."
The representative of the government noted: "We put forward an accurate and credible plan for the formation of our future relations with the EU."
