Forezo-separated buyers will pay more state duty in case of acquisition of real estate in the United Kingdom
Foreign buyers will pay more duties when purchasing real estate in the United Kingdom. In Accordance with Tory's new plans, additional funds will be spent to help the homeless
This event Neutralizes Corbyn's Attempts to Involve Young Soldiers
Above state duty will be levied on those who do not pay taxes in the UK
The Ministry of Finance hopes to attract an additional £120 million a year to help the homeless
As Teresa Mai announced today, foreigners who buy real estate in the UK will pay more duties. Extra funds will be spent on helping the homeless.
This step can be regarded as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn's success of Jeremy Corbyn's attempts to make young voters with more affordable housing aimed at high-incompetitive workers.
Tariff increases will affect individuals and legal entities that don't pay taxes in the United Kingdom. Extended funds will be directed by the government to help those forced to spend the night in the open-eye.
An additional duty, which accompanies an increase of two years ago for a second dwelling and real estate, designed to be leased, can be three percent.
The Ministry of Finance hopes to attract an additional £120 million a year.
According to experts, 13% of new real estate, which is built in London, falls into the ownership of foreign citizens. This leads to an increase in prices and complicates the task for those who only dream of their own housing.
Many affluent districts of the country, in particular, in the capital, are empty, as a large number of foreign buyers most of the time lives outside the country.
The new action plan came just a few weeks after Boris Johnson urged to reduce the state duty to make housing more affordable for young people.
He accuses large construction companies of maintaining high real estate prices by buying up land without its subsequent use. He called on Teresa May to relinquish quotas for affordable real estate to recreate a "shameful position in the housing market" in the UK.
Mr. Corbyn has announced a number of important reforms in the field of real estate, in particular the control of rents and the impossibility of eviction without good reason.
It also proposes to expand the powers of local councils in the construction of new housing.
Teresa May stated: "Last year I promised to dedicate my premiership to the restoration of the British dream - for every new generation of life should be better.
This requires changes in our flammable housing market.
Britain will always remain open to those who strive to live, work and build their future here.
However, it can not be fair that individuals who do not resid in the United Kingdom, as well as foreign companies, buy housing under the same conditions as British citizens.
For too many people, the dream of their own housing has become unattainable. The problem of living in the open air is still very painful."
