Elder-lung heat helped to increase the revenue of British pubs, who are going through not the best of times, but exacerbated pressure on restaurant chains.
In accordance with the calculations, pubs and bars sales increased by 2.7%, while restaurant profits decreased by 4.8%.
The author of the calculations, Peter Martin from the organization CGA, engaged in advising on business development, said: "Thanks to the sunny weather and longer than expected, England's participation in the World World Championship, July passed according to the same scheme as the previous month, when pub sales rose by 2.8%. But in restaurants, things went even worse.
The June fall of 1.8% in July intensified.
Pubs and bars, focused on serving drinks, so far have the best indicators - the growth of their earnings is equivalent to the loss of restaurants.
The pubs in which food are served were also affected by the heat, but not as much as restaurants.
It seems that visitors are only interested in bouncing.
In pubs-controlled pubs and bars sales for the month increased by 6.6%. At the same time, food sales decreased by 3%."
Paul Newman, an analyst at the Department of Permitting and Hospitality at RSM, noted: "Similar trends we have been observing since the end of April.
Weather and large-scale social or sports events remain the main factors affecting sales in the domestic market.
Unsurprisingly, groups of restaurants continue to lose profits. An annual drop in sales of 4.8 percent is of particular concern against the background of the need to reduce costs.
A long hot summer did not come at the best time for establishments serving food. We'll see if a more temperate temperature, which we observed in August, give them such a necessary respite."
A total increase in pubs and restaurants, including new establishments, in July was 2.7%, which reflects a slowdown in the launch of new brands on the market.
A sectoral indicator of Coffer Peach Tracker sales in the UK pubs, bars, and restaurants is a reflection of the collected and analyzed performance indicators of 47 working groups whose total turnover exceeds £9bn sterling. This is a generally accepted industry standard.
